FILE ACCESS: declared outputs are writable. The train 7 per-project snapshot cache (integrated from the previous run: board-data.mjs, watch-server.mjs, command-center-snapshot.mjs) OOMs on the real 9-project board: the watch board process grew to a 4 GB heap and died with 'Reached heap limit' while a Command Center page polled it (5 s poll). The synthetic build-time test passed, but memory is unbounded. Find and fix the retention:
- caches keyed by a changing revision/timestamp or file mtime that adds a new entry on every poll (no reuse/eviction);
- full parsed ledgers/history arrays retained per key; the snapshot cache holding multiple large board snapshots;
- any per-request structure that is never freed.
Bound them: one entry per project/file (replace in place), an LRU or size cap on the snapshot cache with a small number of entries, no retention of full ledgers beyond the cached aggregate, and no per-poll growth. The warm path must stay fast (unchanged project reuse; a ticket edit rebuilds only that project).
Add a memory/leak budget test to tests/board-snapshot-perf.test.mjs or a new test: simulate many polls/revisions (e.g. 100+) with large synthetic ledgers and assert the heap/heapUsed stays bounded (a reasonable cap, e.g. under 200 MB) and the results stay correct; keep the build-time budget. Verify: node --import ./tests/_isolate-config.mjs --test tests/board-snapshot-perf.test.mjs tests/watch-server.test.mjs tests/watch-token-urls.test.mjs tests/command-center-routes-flag.test.mjs tests/command-center-perf-budget.test.mjs -> all pass. Report the leak cause, the bounds, and the measured memory/CPU.
